WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.565
A module that keeps a password or an API key in its settings

00:00:04.565 --> 00:00:09.049
can let users keep it in the Secrets module instead, without depending on it.

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:12.020
Let's see what users get, and the code behind it.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.332
Here are the SMTP email settings, under Settings, Communication, Email.

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:24.387
They follow the pattern this video describes.

00:00:24.387 --> 00:00:28.820
Without the Secrets feature, the Password field is a password input.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:31.187
Let's type a value, and save.

00:00:31.187 --> 00:00:34.840
It's protected with Data Protection and kept in the settings.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.529
The field then says the value is securely stored, and leaving it blank keeps it.

00:00:41.211 --> 00:00:45.524
With the Secrets feature enabled, the same field offers a choice.

00:00:45.524 --> 00:00:49.776
User-specified value keeps the value in the settings, as we saw.

00:00:49.776 --> 00:00:52.631
Use a secret references a secret by name.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.594
Choosing Use a secret lists the text secrets of the site.

00:00:56.594 --> 00:01:00.354
Next to the list are a button that reloads it, and New secret,

00:01:00.354 --> 00:01:04.390
which opens the secret editor in another tab with a suggested name.

00:01:04.851 --> 00:01:06.841
Let's select a secret, and save.

00:01:06.841 --> 00:01:10.560
The settings keep only the name of the secret, and the value that

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.551
was stored with them is removed.

00:01:16.751 --> 00:01:21.144
The OrchardCore.Secrets.Abstractions package provides everything you need:

00:01:21.144 --> 00:01:25.098
an editor model, a tag helper that renders it, a helper that reads

00:01:25.098 --> 00:01:29.491
the value at runtime, and a model for the optional Migrate Secrets screen.

00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:32.202
Add a reference to that package.

00:01:32.202 --> 00:01:35.764
Don't add a dependency on the Secrets feature to your manifest:

00:01:35.764 --> 00:01:39.791
the editor and the runtime helper detect whether the feature is enabled.

00:01:43.991 --> 00:01:48.364
Keep the protected value, as a module without secrets would, and add a

00:01:48.364 --> 00:01:50.666
property for the name of a secret.

00:01:50.666 --> 00:01:53.831
When a secret is referenced, it takes precedence.

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:59.918
Use one constant for the Data Protection purpose, and another for the suggested secret name.

00:01:59.918 --> 00:02:01.607
Never change the purpose:

00:02:01.607 --> 00:02:06.291
a value protected with another purpose can't be decrypted, or moved to a secret.

00:02:10.491 --> 00:02:13.581
The editor model holds a SecretInputViewModel.

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:17.622
It carries the source, which is a value or a secret, the typed

00:02:17.622 --> 00:02:21.961
value, the name of the secret, and whether a value is already kept.

00:02:22.711 --> 00:02:26.856
The display driver of the settings screen calls Create to build the model

00:02:26.856 --> 00:02:28.378
from the stored values.

00:02:28.378 --> 00:02:32.051
The source is Secret when a name is stored, and Value otherwise.

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:37.130
When the settings are saved, the driver calls UpdateAsync on the model.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:41.796
It passes the services, a Data Protection protector, the model state, and the

00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:45.901
full name of the property, so that errors appear next to the field.

00:02:46.651 --> 00:02:51.166
UpdateAsync rejects a secret that doesn't exist, and a switch back to a

00:02:51.166 --> 00:02:52.925
value when none is kept.

00:02:52.925 --> 00:02:56.913
Otherwise it protects a typed value, or keeps the current one.

00:02:56.913 --> 00:02:59.611
Store the result only when it succeeded.

00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:06.431
Register the driver as a site display driver, the way any other settings screen is registered.

00:03:10.631 --> 00:03:14.396
Add the tag helpers of the abstractions to the view imports.

00:03:14.396 --> 00:03:19.075
Then render the property in a standard row with the secret-input tag helper.

00:03:19.075 --> 00:03:23.811
Its secret-name attribute is the name suggested when a user creates a secret.

00:03:24.561 --> 00:03:27.051
The tag helper decides what to render.

00:03:27.051 --> 00:03:30.333
Without the Secrets feature, it is a password input.

00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:34.521
With the feature, it offers the choice between a value and a secret.

00:03:38.721 --> 00:03:42.464
At runtime, GetSecretValueAsync returns the clear value.

00:03:42.464 --> 00:03:47.295
It reads the referenced secret when the Secrets feature is enabled and the

00:03:47.295 --> 00:03:51.461
secret has a value, and otherwise decrypts the protected value.

00:03:52.211 --> 00:03:55.080
It returns null when no value is available.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:59.941
An optional logger receives the reason when a referenced secret can't be read

00:03:59.941 --> 00:04:03.631
or a protected value can't be decrypted, never the value.

00:04:04.381 --> 00:04:09.266
When the value feeds cached options, request an options update when the settings

00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:13.867
change, and register a signal change token source for that options type, as

00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:15.401
the SMTP settings do.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:24.186
Optionally, let users move a stored value to a secret without typing it

00:04:24.186 --> 00:04:26.747
again, on the Migrate Secrets screen.

00:04:26.747 --> 00:04:32.941
Add a display driver of SecretMigration, and register it only when the Secrets feature is enabled.

00:04:33.691 --> 00:04:40.208
Its editor renders a SecretMigrationItem_Edit shape while a value is kept and no secret is referenced.

00:04:40.208 --> 00:04:47.511
Override BuildPrefix, since several drivers edit one migration, and check the permission that guards your settings.

00:04:48.261 --> 00:04:52.905
UpdateAsync moves the selected credential with MoveToSecretAsync.

00:04:52.905 --> 00:04:56.961
It never overwrites a secret, and records a failed move.

00:04:56.961 --> 00:05:02.521
The settings reference the secret only once every driver has saved its secrets.

00:05:06.721 --> 00:05:11.601
The same steps apply to a credential kept anywhere else, such as a content part.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:15.801
Keep the protected value and the secret name side by side, edit them

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:20.000
with a SecretInputViewModel, and read them with GetSecretValueAsync.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.781
Only the driver and the persistence change.

00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:26.490
That's the secret input:

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.879
keep the protected value and the secret name, offer both in the editor,

00:05:30.879 --> 00:05:35.039
and read the value at runtime, with or without the Secrets feature.

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:36.521
Thanks for watching.

